[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Real-Time Communication in .NET: gRPC vs. SignalR"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern web applications frequently require real-time, bi-directional communication capabilities. In the .NET ecosystem, two primary technologies stand out for this purpose: gRPC and ASP.NET Core SignalR. While both enable real-time data flow, they are architected for fundamentally different use cases and system boundaries."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding ASP.NET Core SignalR"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"SignalR is a library for ASP.NET Core developers that simplifies adding real-time web functionality to applications. It abstracts underlying transport layers—such as WebSockets, Server-Sent Events, and Long Polling—and automatically falls back to the best available option. SignalR is heavily optimized for client-to-server and server-to-client communication within web browsers and mobile clients."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a SignalR Hub"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To set up SignalR in a .NET backend, you define a Hub class that inherits from Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Hub. Below is an example of a notification hub:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"using Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR;

public class NotificationHub : Hub
{
    public async Task SendMessage(string user, string message)
    {
        await Clients.All.SendAsync("ReceiveMessage", user, message);
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding gRPC in .NET"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"gRPC is a high-performance, open-source universal RPC (Remote Procedure Call) framework initially developed by Google. Built on HTTP/2, it uses Protocol Buffers (protobuf) as its interface definition language and serialization format. .NET provides first-class support for gRPC, making it ideal for microservices and backend-to-backend communication requiring high throughput and low latency."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining a gRPC Service"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"gRPC contracts are defined using Protocol Buffers in a .proto file. Here is an example defining a streaming data service:"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"syntax = "proto3";

package telemetry;

service TelemetryService {
    rpc StreamTelemetry (TelemetryRequest) returns (stream TelemetryResponse);
}

message TelemetryRequest {
    string device_id = 1;
}

message TelemetryResponse {
    double temperature = 1;
    int64 timestamp = 2;
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Architectural Differences"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Protocol: SignalR uses JSON (by default via MessagePack option) over HTTP transport abstractions. gRPC strictly enforces HTTP/2 and binary serialization using Protocol Buffers."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Target Audience: SignalR is built for browser-to-server and browser-to-client scenarios. gRPC excels in server-to-server microservice communication."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Contract Definition: SignalR uses standard C# interfaces and dynamic method invocation. gRPC relies on strict contract-first .proto files generating strongly-typed client and server code."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion and Best Practices"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choose SignalR when building interactive web or mobile frontends that require automatic transport fallback and broadcast capabilities. Choose gRPC when building internal microservices in .NET where serialization efficiency, strict schemas, and raw performance over HTTP/2 are paramount."}]
